readcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Carpentry","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Crown Molding, Trim and Wainscoting","item":"https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry/trim-crown-and-wainscoting"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","serviceType":"Crown Molding, Trim and Wainscoting","url":"https://mcitybuilders.com/carpentry/trim-crown-and-wainscoting","provider":{"@id":"https://mcitybuilders.com/#business"},"areaServed":{"@type":"AdministrativeArea","name":"Central Massachusetts"}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Should crown molding be coped or mitered at inside corners?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Coped. Very few room corners are exactly 90 degrees, and a mitered inside corner opens up as the house moves through the seasons. A coped joint, where one piece is cut to the profile of the other, stays tight much longer. Outside corners are mitered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is MDF trim good enough?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For painted crown, casing and panels in dry rooms, yes. It is smooth, stable and cheaper. It does not like water, so we avoid it for baseboard in bathrooms, mudrooms and basements, where poplar, pine or PVC trim holds up better."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How tall should wainscoting be?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A common rule is about a third of the wall height, which lands at 32 to 36 inches on an 8-foot ceiling. Taller paneling, up to 5 or 6 feet, works in mudrooms, stair halls and rooms with high ceilings. We mock up heights on the wall before we build."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you match the original trim in an old house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, yes. Common profiles are still available, and a local mill can run knives to copy an old profile when nothing off the shelf is close. Matching takes a sample piece and some lead time, so plan for it early."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do trim joints crack in winter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Wood shrinks as the heating season dries out the house. Some hairline movement at painted joints is normal in New England. Good joinery, glued and pinned miters, and trim installed at normal living temperature and humidity keep it to a minimum."}}]}</script><!-- First-touch attribution. Runs before anything else so a click-id is never
